<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Judicate of Logudoro</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1259 CE (approx.) · capital Ardara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern Sardinian kingdom of Torres, one of four native judicates that ruled the island after Byzantine authority faded and a patron of Sardinia's finest Romanesque churches.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As Byzantine control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> lapsed in the 10th and early 11th centuries, the island divided into four self-governing realms ruled by judges (judikes). Logudoro, also called Torres after the old Roman port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-torres">Porto Torres</a>, covered the north-west. Its rulers held court at Ardara and appear in documents from the early 11th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The judge ruled with an assembly of notables, the corona de logu, and governed through curatorie, rural districts. Land registers called condaghes were kept in Sardinian, among the earliest texts in the language. Judges invited Benedictine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camaldolese">Camaldolese</a> and Vallombrosan monks, who built churches such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santissima-trinita-di-saccargia">Santissima Trinità di Saccargia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> competed for its trade and favour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelasia-of-torres">Adelasia</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enzo-of-sardinia">Enzo</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Emperor Frederick II</a>, but died without heirs in 1259. The realm was then carved up between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doria-family">Doria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaspina">Malaspina</a> families, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/judicate-of-arborea">Judicate of Arborea</a> and the town of Sassari, which became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-sassari">Republic of Sassari</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/judicate-of-logudoro.jpg" alt="Judicate of Logudoro" width="320" height="626"><figcaption>Image: Facquis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Judicate_of_Torres_(XI-XIV_centuries).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1259 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ardara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gonario II of Torres, Barisone II of Torres, Adelasia of Torres</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sardinian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Judicate of Torres, Giudicato di Torres</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1130&amp;focus=judicate-of-logudoro" aria-label="Judicate of Logudoro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4886 720 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Judicate of Logudoro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5206 976L5210 979M5206 976L5212 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5210 979h0M5212 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5206 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5206 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1130&amp;focus=judicate-of-logudoro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
